- Retrieved practices:
- Do not treat device paths as regular files
WHAT: Performing file operations (open, read, write, delete, etc.) on device paths that are only appropriate for devices (e.g., reserved names) instead of regular files.
WHY: Misusing device paths can cause denial of service, data corruption, or unexpected system behavior by interacting directly with system devices.
HOW: Detect and reject or specially handle reserved device names/paths before performing file I/O; maintain an allowlist of valid file path patterns and ensure user-controlled paths cannot reference device names.
EXAMPLE: (C) Before: `f = fopen(user_path, "w");` After: `if (is_valid_file_path(user_path) && !is_reserved_device_name(user_path)) { f = fopen(user_path, "w"); }`

- Avoid invoking system() or equivalent command processors
WHAT: Calling system(), popen(), _popen(), or similar APIs to execute commands with strings that may be influenced by untrusted input can allow arbitrary command execution, especially when using unsanitized arguments, missing or relative paths, or spoofable executables.
WHY: Attackers can inject or alter commands to run arbitrary programs with the privileges of the process, leading to code execution, data compromise, or full system takeover.
HOW: Do not use system() or command-processor-based APIs; instead, use safer process-execution APIs that bypass the shell, pass arguments as separate parameters, require absolute paths to executables, and validate all user-controlled inputs with strict allowlists.
EXAMPLE (C): // Before: vulnerable
// system(cmdbuf);  // cmdbuf includes user-controlled input
// After: safer (POSIX)
// execl("/usr/bin/any_cmd", "any_cmd", validated_arg, (char *)NULL);

- Correct use of errno in C error handling
WHAT: Misusing errno by reading it without first checking a function’s failure return value, or by not resetting errno to 0 before calling functions that may set it, can cause incorrect error detection and handling.
WHY: Stale or unrelated errno values can lead to misreported errors, skipped error handling, or incorrect program behavior, potentially masking real failures.
HOW: For functions documented to set errno on error, first check their return value for failure; only if it indicates failure, then inspect errno. When using errno to detect errors for functions without a unique error return, set errno to 0 immediately before the call, then check errno only after the call completes.
EXAMPLE: Before: `f = fopen(path, "r"); if (errno == ENOENT) { ... }`
After: `errno = 0; f = fopen(path, "r"); if (f == NULL) { if (errno == ENOENT) { ... } }`

- Detect and handle standard library errors
WHAT: Failing to check or incorrectly checking standard library function return values (including NULL returns) can leave errors undetected or unhandled.
WHY: Unhandled errors can cause undefined behavior, crashes (e.g., NULL pointer dereference), security bypasses, or data corruption.
HOW: For every standard library call that can fail, always check its documented return value or error indicator, handle all failure cases explicitly, and avoid using results (pointers, sizes, descriptors) until they are validated.
EXAMPLE (C): Before: `char *p = malloc(n); memcpy(p, src, n);` After: `char *p = malloc(n); if (p == NULL) { /* handle error */ return ERR; } memcpy(p, src, n);`

- Guarantee sufficient storage for strings and prevent sprintf() buffer overflows
WHAT: Failing to allocate enough space for strings (including the null terminator) or using sprintf() without bounding the output can cause buffer overflows when handling external or user-controlled strings.
WHY: Buffer overflows can corrupt memory, crash the program, or enable arbitrary code execution.
HOW: Always allocate string buffers with space for all characters plus the null terminator, and when formatting strings, use bounded operations or precision specifiers so the total written bytes never exceed the buffer size.
EXAMPLE: Noncompliant (C): `char filename[128]; sprintf(filename, "%s.txt", name);`
Compliant (C): `char filename[128]; sprintf(filename, "%.123s.txt", name);`

- Do not read uninitialized memory or overflow fixed-size buffers in C error-reporting code
WHAT: Using uninitialized pointers (for example, `const char *error_log;` without assignment) or writing formatted strings into fixed-size buffers with `sprintf` can read indeterminate memory and overflow the buffer.
WHY: Reading uninitialized memory can leak arbitrary data and cause undefined behavior; overflowing a stack buffer can lead to crashes or code execution.
HOW: Always initialize local pointers and variables before use; when copying or formatting into fixed-size buffers, use bounded functions (for example, `snprintf`) and ensure the buffer is large enough for all content plus the null terminator.
EXAMPLE: Before (unsafe): `const char *error_log; char buf[24]; sprintf(buf, "Error: %s", error_log);` After (safer): `const char *error_log = msg; char buf[24]; snprintf(buf, sizeof buf, "Error: %s", error_log);`



